      <h2>Airlines Flying Nonstop from New York to Miami</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>American Airlines</strong> — Dominant on this route with multiple daily nonstop flights from JFK, LGA, and EWR to MIA</li>
        <li><strong>Delta Air Lines</strong> — Multiple daily from JFK and LGA to MIA and FLL (Fort Lauderdale)</li>
        <li><strong>JetBlue</strong> — Multiple daily from JFK to MIA, with Mint premium options on select services</li>
        <li><strong>Spirit Airlines</strong> — Budget nonstop from LGA to MIA, often the cheapest available fare</li>
        <li><strong>Frontier Airlines</strong> — Budget nonstop service with low base fares</li>
        <li><strong>United</strong> — Daily service from EWR to MIA</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Tips for New York to Miami Nonstop</h2>
      <p>This is one of the easiest routes to find a cheap nonstop fare on, given the intense competition. Spirit and Frontier consistently undercut legacy carriers on base price. For a 3-hour flight, the trade-off is often worth it for budget travelers.</p>
      <div class="info-box"><p>ℹ️ Fort Lauderdale (FLL) is 30 minutes from Miami by car and served by even more budget carriers. If you are staying in Miami Beach, FLL is often cheaper and nearly as convenient. Always compare FLL fares alongside MIA when searching.</p></div>
      <ul>
        <li>Book at least 3 weeks ahead for winter holiday travel — New York to Miami spikes in December and March (spring break).</li>
        <li>Spirit and Frontier fares look cheap but add bag and seat fees — calculate the total cost before booking.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Terminal Guide: New York and Miami</h2>
      <p><strong>At New York JFK:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Terminal 8 (T8):</strong> American Airlines — AA's main JFK terminal for both domestic and international departures.</li>
        <li><strong>Terminal 4 (T4):</strong> Delta Air Lines domestic JFK departures.</li>
        <li><strong>Terminal 5 (T5):</strong> JetBlue's home terminal at JFK — generally considered the best domestic terminal in New York.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>At New York LaGuardia (LGA):</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Terminal B:</strong> American Airlines at LGA. The newest terminal at LaGuardia, modern with good dining options.</li>
        <li><strong>Terminal C:</strong> Delta Air Lines at LGA.</li>
        <li><strong>Terminal A:</strong> Spirit Airlines and other budget carriers.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>At Miami International Airport (MIA):</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>MIA has a single integrated terminal building organized into three concourses — D (north), E (central/international), and H/J (south). American Airlines dominates MIA and operates from multiple concourses. Delta, JetBlue, and other carriers use assigned concourse gates within the integrated terminal. MIA uses a people-mover (MIA Mover) to connect to the rental car facility and Miami Central Station.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="tip-box"><p>💡 Fort Lauderdale-Hollywood International (FLL) is a separate airport approximately 30 miles north of Miami. Uber and Lyft from FLL to South Beach or downtown Miami takes 40–60 minutes and costs $35–60. If the FLL fare is $50+ cheaper than MIA, the ground transfer cost may still make FLL the better deal.</p></div>
      <h2>Baggage Allowances by Airline</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>American Airlines:</strong> Carry-on and personal item included. First checked bag $35 each way on domestic economy fares unless you hold AAdvantage status or a co-branded Citi/Barclays credit card.</li>
        <li><strong>Delta:</strong> Carry-on included. First checked bag $35 unless you hold Medallion status or Delta credit card.</li>
        <li><strong>JetBlue:</strong> One carry-on and personal item included. First checked bag $35 on Blue and below; free from Blue Extra and above.</li>
        <li><strong>Spirit Airlines:</strong> A personal item (under the seat) is included in the base fare. A standard carry-on costs extra ($39–79 each way depending on when purchased). Checked bags are also extra. Always purchase bags at booking — Spirit's gate bag fees are the highest in the industry.</li>
        <li><strong>Frontier:</strong> Similar to Spirit — personal item only included on base fare. Carry-on and checked bags are purchased add-ons.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Best Time to Book New York to Miami Nonstop</h2>
      <p><strong>Cheapest periods:</strong> Early September through October (after summer, before snowbird season). Mid-January (post-holiday). Mid-April through May (after spring break). Midweek departures (Tuesday, Wednesday) save 15–20% versus weekend flights year-round.</p>
      <p><strong>Most expensive:</strong> March (spring break peak). Thanksgiving week. Christmas and New Year's. December through early January (snowbird season peak). Presidents' Day weekend.</p>
      <p><strong>Booking window:</strong> Domestic fares on this route are typically best 3–6 weeks in advance. Fare alerts on Google Flights or Hopper are effective for monitoring this high-volume route where prices change frequently.</p>
      <h2>Visa and Entry Requirements</h2>
      <p>This is a domestic US route — no passport or visa is required. A valid government-issued photo ID (driver's license, state ID, or passport) is required for TSA security. From May 2025, Real ID-compliant identification is required at all US airports for domestic travel. Verify your state's compliance before traveling.</p>
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <h3>Is it worth flying from Newark (EWR) to Miami instead of JFK or LGA?</h3>
      <p>EWR adds travel time from Manhattan (45–60 minutes) versus LGA (30–45 minutes) but has United Airlines service to MIA and sometimes cheaper fares. If you are a United MileagePlus member or live in New Jersey, EWR is a natural choice. For most Manhattan residents, LGA or JFK are more convenient starting points.</p>
      <h3>How long does the nonstop flight from New York to Miami take?</h3>
      <p>Approximately 3 hours and 5–20 minutes, depending on routing and winds. The return (Miami to New York) takes about the same — wind effects are minimal on this north-south corridor. Block time (including taxi) is typically 3.5 hours total from gate to gate.</p>
      <h3>Should I fly to MIA or FLL for Miami Beach?</h3>
      <p>For Miami Beach specifically, MIA is closer — 25–30 minutes by taxi or rideshare versus 45–60 from FLL. But if FLL is more than $40 cheaper round-trip per person, the price difference typically offsets the extra ground transfer cost. For families or groups, MIA's convenience advantage grows because ground transportation costs multiply by headcount.</p>
      <h3>Does JetBlue Mint operate New York to Miami?</h3>
      <p>JetBlue operates Mint (business class with lie-flat seats) on select JFK–MIA flights, primarily on A321 aircraft. Not all JFK–MIA flights have Mint — check the seat map at booking. When Mint is available, prices typically run $250–500 each way, well below other carriers' premium cabin prices on this route.</p>
      <h2>In-Flight Experience Comparison</h2>
      <p><strong>American Airlines Economy:</strong> Standard domestic product on A321 or 737. Main Cabin Extra (extra legroom) available as paid upgrade. Complimentary beverage service, no meals on 3-hour domestic flights. Wi-Fi available for purchase.</p>
      <p><strong>Delta Economy:</strong> Standard domestic product. Comfort+ (extra legroom) available. Complimentary snack and beverage. Delta's domestic on-time performance is generally strong on this route. SkyMiles earning on all paid fares.</p>
      <p><strong>JetBlue Economy:</strong> Most legroom of any domestic carrier (32-inch minimum pitch across the cabin). Free Wi-Fi on all flights. Free snacks and beverages. Even More Space upgrade available. Best standard economy product on this domestic route for most travelers.</p>
      <p><strong>Spirit Economy:</strong> Bare-bones product — the seat, one small personal item, and minimal crew service included. Aircraft is typically A320 family. No entertainment, no complimentary refreshments. Suitable for very price-sensitive travelers for whom the total cost including bag fees is still below alternatives.</p>
